Each step names the item it uses: "my #4". A step with no item is "my reading".

I need to be careful: Sun Tzu's rules are about warfare/decision, applied as a lens. The brief's house rules: every check must be able to fail, shown failing once on a planted wrong input; never fill missing threshold with invented number; trigger is a comparison; MUST only for safety bound; no canary; never print a secret; skill runs in other projects so nothing project-specific hardcoded.

Let me structure the verdict per requirement. My list has 20 items. Relevant:
1. Reckon before battle (count/compare before committing) — relevant to R1 context, R3 planning.
2. Make yourself unbeatable first; wait — active/relevant to readiness: build own readiness (verified), enemy's move is not yours to control. "Do not rely on their not coming; rely on having what cannot be attacked." That maps to R6/R7, R2 tools.
3. Whole over broken; strike plans first, walled cities last — prefer less-forceful target; relevant to verification design? Maybe a review of ordered targets: cheap checks before expensive ones? Actually #3's ranking is about striking plans first, cities last. For a harness health check: prefer the cheap indirect fault (config) vs the expensive (dispatch). Hmm.
4. Know whether you can fight; know the other side and yourself — readiness is exactly this: know yourself (what's installed), know the enemy? In harness health, the "enemy" is the failure mode. Knowing five ways. Relevant to R11 at readiness vs post-step-2.
5. Move only on advantage; never from anger — threshold: don't add a requirement without the gain; don't move on dislike of a clause.
6. Mix harm into benefit — each requirement's benefit must be judged with its harm; reviewers must know the harm before benefit. And "whoever does not fully know the harm of using troops cannot fully know its benefit" — apply to each requirement: ask what it costs to run.
7. War is the way of deception — the harness failures "looked like success (exit 0)". The enemy (failure) conceals itself: appear able when not. This is a key insight for the harness health check: exit 0 is deception; must check the output, not the exit code. Also relevant to prompt injection defenses (R6). But my #7 says "attack where they are unprepared" — the check should attack the harness's blind spot.
8. Value victory, not duration; speed — R11.2 first signal: fast feedback. "clumsy speed" vs "skillful prolongation" — a slow judge is a liability; but not any speed. Also R7 retries (long campaign blunts). Relevant to Q timing.
9. Bring the other side to you; do not be brought — for readiness: the check sets the ground (runs commands itself), doesn't rely on the harness. Also "first seize what he cherishes" — target what matters. Relevant to R5 verifier independence? Maybe not.
10. Avoid the solid, strike the empty; no constant form — a health check must not have a fixed form; attack the gaps the config readers don't read (the 7 behavior failures). "Preparing everywhere, he is few everywhere" — a check trying to cover all 11 requirements shallowly is thin; concentrate. Relevant to where to put the check.
11. Foreknowledge must come from people; pay for it — readiness must get knowledge from people who know (run commands, logs), not from precedent or measurement alone. "cannot be verified by measurement (度)" — config files are 度? Actually "不可驗於度" means cannot be verified by measurement/precedent; must come from people who know the enemy's situation. This directly attacks the built check: it reads config files only ("reported") and cannot get at the true situation, which is the dispatched behavior. Also don't begrudge the cost of a canary/spend — but house rule says no canary without EJ's go-ahead. So #11 says pay for foreknowledge (a small paid call is worth it compared to a ruined multi-year campaign), but the binding house rule overrides: no canary without EJ. That's a real tension worth naming. Also #11's "not from spirits, not from likeness to past events" — a check must not rely on precedent ("this worked last time").
12. Seek it in 勢 (regular + irregular), not blame people — build the ground so correct behavior is easy; the harness failures are position/fault of the harness, not the agent's character. Relevant to R10 feedback loop: fix the harness, not exhort the agent.
13. Not every road is taken, and not every command of the ruler is accepted — requirements should not be MUSTs blindly; the general may refuse. Relevant to R6/R8: rules that can't be checked at readiness shouldn't be forced into the readiness list. Also "there are roads not to be taken" — a health check should decline to check what it cannot check, rather than claim.
14. Choose action by ratio of forces — a check's depth should match the strength: 10:1 surround, 1:1 hold. For requirements: don't spend more check than the failure is worth; small stubborn force is captured. Relevant to sizing checks and to the 80% count.
15. General's faults: five dangers — each virtue is a handle. The harness health check has its own faults: e.g., "MUST" list can be gamed; "loves the people" (over-cautious check that never fails); "set on dying" (a check that always fails). Each requirement could itself be a handle for the opposite failure. Relevant to R11.2 and the checks being able to fail.
16. Orders clear first, then enforced — requirements must be clear and drilled (checked) before they're held against anyone. R11 is unclear (Q1/Q2). If the rule isn't clear, it's the requirement author's fault, not the check's. Relevant.
17. Learn the enemy by probing and signs — the harness health check should probe (run commands) and read signs (outputs), not just config. Also "humble words with more preparation mean advance" etc. — signs in logs.
18. Spirit/heart/strength/change — ignore the flagging tool? Hmm. "avoid keen spirit, strike when it flags" — an unverified check? Maybe relevant to timing: run the check when? "Eight prescriptions" including leave a gap for a surrounded army, don't press a desperate enemy — relevant to R7 retries: leave a gap, don't press a failing engine. Relevant to R6 permission: leave a gap.
19. Name the ground, act as prescribed — readiness's four labels (verified/reported/unknown/MISSING) are exactly "name the ground, then act": each ground has a prescribed action. This is a strong mapping. "troops with no way out fight" — a check with no escape (a hard gate) fights. Also a requirement list should be a table from condition to response, not a fixed sequence.
20. Which house falls first: judge by land unit, tax, and what they breed — measure structural cause (unit size, tax rate), not symptom. For a harness health check: judge by the structural configuration that predicts failure, not by the failure itself. Also the closing line is the king's, not Sunzi's — attribute correctly (the requirements' author unknown — don't put words in the author's mouth / label). This is a strong metaphor: the health check judged by the "tax unit" (how small the unit, how heavy the tax) — i.e., structural metrics predict which harness falls. Relevant to R9 observability and to the backtest (config predicts 0 of 7 behavior failures — so config is the wrong unit).

Now R1-R11 review with keep/change/split/drop and readiness checkability.

Let me think about the overall answer. The key strategic insight from the list: 
- The requirements mostly describe "being", not "doing" — they are prescriptions without a check, so at readiness they can only be labelled, not verified. Per my #13 (roads not taken) and #19, readiness should label each requirement's state (verified/reported/unknown/MISSING) rather than pretend to enforce.
- The 7 behavior failures all looked like success (exit 0). My #7 deception and #17 signs: a config-only check is foreknowledge from 度 (measurement), which 用間 forbids — must come from people/behavior. So the health check must run a probe at dispatch, not read config.
- R11.1's 80% over prompts vs acceptance criteria: Q1.
- R11.2 first signal: my #8 (speed) supports it, and my #14 (ratio of forces) defines "fast" as relative to the judged thing.

Let me go through each requirement carefully.

R1 Clear context delivery. Keep. Checkable at readiness? Partially. A check that can fail: does an instruction file exist at the expected path and is it non-empty; is its size within a budget? But "treat the window as a budget" — the budget number is not given → cannot check without an invented threshold (house rule). So change: split the checkable part (file exists, is read, is loaded) from the unmeasurable part (is it "good"). My #1 says count before commit; my #14 says numbers not valued for their own sake — size is not the goal. So R1's "budget not dumping ground" needs a comparison, not a number. A failing check: feed the harness an instruction file that does not exist / is empty / is not loaded into the first prompt → the check must fail. That is testable at readiness only if we can inspect the prompt actually assembled — which spends no quota if the harness echoes context? Hmm. Actually "readiness" proves what's installed and logged in, spends no model quota. So checking R1 at readiness = "the file exists and is at a discoverable path" (verified by reading the filesystem), not "the agent received it". To prove delivery needs a dispatch (quota). So R1: keep, readiness check = instruction file found and non-empty (can fail), but note it proves presence, not delivery — label accordingly. My #17/#11 (probe and signs) says delivery is proven by the agent's output, which is post-readiness.

R2 Well-designed tools. Keep, but not checkable at readiness as stated — "simple, unambiguous, error messages that tell how to fix, explicit boundaries" is a property of a tool's whole behavior, not its config. Readiness could check: each tool has a declared name and a schema/description; an invalid call returns a message naming the invalid field. That second one requires calling the tool (a dispatch, possibly quota, maybe not if it's a local tool). Split: (a) declared interface check — readable at readiness (can fail: a tool with no description); (b) error-message quality — must be probed by a planted bad call (my #17: "test him in contact (角)"), and the planted call must be made to fail once before the check is trusted (house rule). My #15 warns: a tool that never errors can't be tested — the "loving the people" fault. So split R2.

R3 Planning and task structure. Keep. Checkable at readiness? The done-criteria exists per task — but tasks aren't defined yet at readiness (readiness is step 1, before the algorithm step 2). So R3 belongs to step 2's output, not readiness. Readiness could check "a task-list template/format exists". A failing check: a task with no written done-criterion → the check fails. That is post-step-2. My #1 (reckon first) and #8 (don't prolong): done-criteria is the count before committing. Keep, move check to step 2. My #12 (choose people, rely on 勢): a structure that depends on the agent's diligence rather than a required field is the wrong design.

R4 State and memory across sessions. Keep, but change: make it a check on a handoff artifact, not on "memory directories exist". Readiness can check the directory/paths exist (verified), cannot check that a fresh context can resume. The real check is a drill: a fresh session, given only the state file, names the next step — that's a dispatch. Actually there is a cheap version: take the last state file and ask whether it names the next action and its evidence path; a human or a rule can check this without quota. My #1/#4: know yourself. A failing check: state file's last entry is older than the last completed step, or names no next action. My #16: orders clear first. So R4 keep, readiness = paths exist (reported), real check = state file names next action and the step it refers to exists.

R5 Independent verification. Keep — this is the core. Change: state it as "the builder does not judge its own work" and require a named verifier per task. Checkable at readiness? Partially: readiness can check the verifier exists as a tool/command/agent. Cannot check it is independent or effective. My #2 (make yourself unbeatable) and #11 (foreknowledge from people): evidence over claim — the check must be on the output, not the claim. Also my #15: a verifier that always passes (loving the people) is a fault. My #14: verification depth should match the risk. So R5 keep; check at step 2 = every task names its verifier, and readiness = the verifier's command exists and runs. Can fail: a task whose verifier is the same agent/engine as the builder.

R6 Guardrails and permissions. Keep — but per house rule "MUST only for a safety bound", this is one of the few that can be MUST. Readiness checkable? Yes partly: config keys exist (deny rules, sandbox mode, approval policy) — that's the built check. But note: config is `reported`, not `verified`; the built check itself says "configured is not enforced". My #17 (probe) and #7 (deception): the only way to verify a guardrail is to attempt the forbidden action in a safe way and observe the denial. That may not be possible at readiness without a canary/quota; for local sandbox, a planted write outside the allowed path can be attempted with no quota. So R6: keep; readiness = read config (reported) + one planted denial probe where cheap. My #13 (roads not taken): never let the health check edit config — advise only (house rule). My #15: the check's own permission is a fault — if the health check runs with broad access, it's the "loving the people" problem.

R7 Recovery and retries. Keep, change. "Idempotent, rollback points, sensible retry limits" — "sensible" is a weak word with no number; house rule: never invent the number; trigger is a comparison. So make it: each dispatched step declares (a) whether re-running it is safe, (b) its rollback point, (c) its retry limit compared to what? A retry limit is a number EJ must set, or compare to the step's cost. Readiness cannot check idempotence of a step that hasn't run; it can check the declaration exists. My #8 (value victory, not duration; speed): retries prolong; a long campaign blunts. My #18 #8: "do not press a desperate enemy; leave a gap for a surrounded army" — a retry limit must leave an escape/stop condition. My #2: don't rely on the step not failing. So R7 keep; readiness = the dispatch wrapper declares retry limit and rollback; a planted failing step must exhaust the limit and stop, not loop.

R8 Human-in-the-loop gates. Keep. Readiness = the approval policy is set and the bypass prompt is not skipped (built check finds it skipped). But this is config = reported. Real check: a planted action that requires approval actually blocks (probe). My #13: the ruler's command not accepted — a gate must allow the operator to refuse/override. My #16: clear first. My #5: don't move without advantage — gates should sit only where judgment is needed, not everywhere (EJ's words: people handle only calls needing judgment). A failing check: a risky action proceeds without approval → check fails.

R9 Observability. Keep, but change from "log every action, token and cost, decision point and anomaly" — "every" is a universal that almost nothing can fail against without exhaustive coverage. My #1: count, but count what matters. Split: (a) each dispatch writes a log with the fields that let a failure be found (exit code, command, duration, engine, prompt id, cost if any); (b) anomalies are visible. Checkable at readiness: no — needs a prior run's logs. Check: run one no-quota action and confirm a log line appears with the fields. A failing check: a run whose log lacks the engine or the output — and the 7 failures show exit 0 with no output, so the log must capture output, not just exit status. My #17 signs: read the signs, not the claim. My #7: exit 0 is deception.

R10 Feedback loop that improves the harness. Keep. "Every agent mistake becomes a permanent fix." Checkable at readiness? No — it's a property of the process over time. Readiness can check a register/ledger file exists and has an entry per recorded failure. A failing check: a recorded failure with no rule/check/tool linked → fail. My #10 (no constant form) and #20 (judge by the unit/tax): review the scaffold as models improve — some scaffolding exists to compensate for current weaknesses; my #12 says move the ground, not blame people. My #1 (reckon): a feedback entry must name the failure, the fix, and how the fix is checked. My #15: a check that can't fail is a fault.

R11.1 80% of prompts have their result judged by a pre-existing rule/check/test/eval/standard. Change — needs definitions (Q1, Q2) and a trigger comparison. Not checkable at readiness as written, because prompts don't exist at readiness; it is checkable at step 2's exit (per the conflict note and my #1/#19: name the ground then act). But readiness can list the judges (my #1's count of your own resources). A failing check: a step with no named judge → the step becomes an unclear spot (as the method already says). Note: the 80% is n=0, not measured; per house rule, do not enforce a number that isn't defined. Change: define the denominator (Q1), define judge (Q2), and count at step 2. Also per my #14: "more is not what is valued" — 80% is not the goal; every catastrophic step should be judged, and cheap low-risk steps may not need it. Actually careful: the 80% is EJ's number; I should not override it, but I can note it lacks a basis and ask.

R11.2 (SHOULD) first signal before full verdict; a fast check that can fail on its own; full verdict after; expected duration written; exception with reason. Keep — this matches my #8 (speed) and my #17 (probe) and #14 (proportion). "Fast" is defined by comparison — good, that's the house rule (a trigger is a comparison). Checkable at readiness? The judge's declaration of its first check and duration is readable at readiness (per the design), so yes as a plan — but whether the first check actually runs first is only verified by watching it run. A failing check: a judge that produces a full verdict without its declared first check, or a first check slower than the judged prompt. Change: make "can fail the result on its own" the testable core (planted wrong input → fail). My #15: a first check that never fails is a fault. My #18: don't press the enemy — the first check should be cheap.

Q1: 80% over prompts or acceptance criteria? My answer conditionally, through my #1 and #14 and #19. My reading: the unit should be the thing that can fail — the acceptance criterion, because that's what "judged" means and where a judge attaches. But R5's unit is the task/prompt. Hmm. Let me think: a prompt can cover several criteria; a criterion can span prompts. The thing a judge returns pass/fail on is the criterion (or the deliverable). Counting over prompts lets a prompt with 5 criteria and 1 judge count as judged; over criteria it counts 1/5. My #1 (reckon with more/fewer 算) and #19 (name the ground, act as prescribed — the unit defines the prescription) say: pick the unit that the judge actually returns a verdict on. If the judge returns a verdict on a prompt's output as a whole, count prompts; if it returns verdicts per criterion, count criteria. But that makes the denominator depend on the judge, which is circular. So my recommendation: define the unit as the acceptance criterion, because that is where pass/fail lives and it is the finer unit; con: more bookkeeping, and the number falls (a prompt with one judge covering 5 criteria counts 20%, not 100%). But per my #14 "numbers are not valued for their own sake" — the number's purpose is to find the unjudged piece, and criteria-level finds more. However I should not decide for EJ; I answer conditionally and list the deciding fact: which unit the design already numbers tasks by (the method's step 2 is per step, so prompts). Actually the method's step 2 numbers algorithm steps, each naming its check. So the natural unit in the method is the step. Hmm — the method says "every step must name its check". That's per-step, and step ≈ prompt. Given the method already attaches checks to steps, counting over steps is cheaper; but acceptance criteria are the pass/fail unit. I'll answer: if the goal is to catch an unjudged result, use acceptance criteria, because a prompt's "result" is judged criterion by criterion; if the goal is to catch an unjudged prompt, use prompts. And ask EJ which failure they want to catch — that changes the unit. That's honest and follows "if … then …".

Q2: Does any design-standard document count as a judge, or only something that can return pass or fail? My #4 (know whether you can fight — knowing how to use many and few), my #13 (not every road), my #16 (clear first then enforced), my #15 (a virtue as a handle). Answer: only something that can return pass or fail — because the house rule says every check must be able to fail, and a document that cannot fail is a reference, not a judge. My #11 (foreknowledge from people): a standard is 度/measurement unless a person applies it to return a verdict. So: a design-standard counts only when paired with a named human or rule that reads the result against it and can say fail. Otherwise it's context (R1), not a judge. That answers Q2 crisply. Per my #1, the standard must be named up front (before the prompt) — matches R11.1's "exists before the prompt is sent".

What the list misses — N1, N2...

Looking at the 7 failures and the requirements, the gaps:
- N1: A check on the output, not the exit code — "success that is not success." The 7 failures looked like exit 0 with empty output. No requirement says "a dispatched result must carry evidence that the work happened." R5 says evidence not claim, but at the *builder's* level; what's missing is a per-dispatch output check that can fail on empty/no-op output (agy soft-deny, opencode auto-reject). Failure caught: a task marked done with an empty or unchanged output. This is my #7/#17.
- N2: Dispatch environment/transport requirements — stdin redirected (codex hang), concurrency limits (opencode database lock), worktree write policy (heredoc refusals). The list has nothing about the *dispatch mechanics*: how work is handed to an engine and what preconditions each engine needs. Failure caught: a hang, a lock collision, a refused write. My #1 (count before commit — know the ground) and #14 (ratio of forces — don't start two against one database) and #17 (probe).
- N3: Idempotent re-dispatch for engines that write — R7 covers idempotence of steps but not parallel-safety / shared resource locking. Actually that overlaps N2. Maybe make N3: a liveness/timeout requirement — every dispatch has a deadline after which it is killed and reported, because codex hang and 502 show a hung dispatch can look like slow work. My #8 (speed) and #18 (do not press).
- N4: Cost/quota accounting per dispatch (R9 says log tokens and cost; but no requirement caps or budgets quota; "no canary without EJ's go-ahead"). The list misses a rule on when spending is allowed and who approves it. My #11 (pay for foreknowledge) vs house rule. Failure caught: quota spent by an unapproved canary / run over budget. Actually R9 logs cost; a budget is different. Good N.
- N5: An explicit "unknown/MISSING" handling rule — what the workflow does when readiness can't prove a requirement. agy/qwen/dsh/opencode are unknown for R6/R8. The list has no requirement on what to do with an unverifiable line: block, warn, or proceed with a named risk. My #13 (not every road is taken) and #19 (name the ground and act as prescribed — the four labels are the ground, but there's no prescription attached to `unknown`). Failure caught: work dispatched to an engine whose guardrails were never verified, silently. That's a real gap and directly answers "can it be checked at readiness".
- N6: The list is about a single harness, but the design dispatches to six engines; nothing requires per-engine capability/contract (which engine can do what, read-only form, structured output). EXECUTOR_KINDS exists in the project, but the requirement list misses "the engine's known failure modes are written and checked before dispatch". Failure caught: agy plan mode stops at a plan; opencode rejects outside-repo files. My #17 (learn the enemy by probing).
- N7: Feedback from *other* projects / the field — R10 is a loop but the list has no requirement that a check is portable (skill runs in other projects, nothing project-specific hardcoded). Hmm, that's a house rule, and R10 says review the harness as models improve. The missed item: each check names what it does not establish and who wrote it — no, that's the project's SHOULD. Maybe N7: a requirement that a check be shown failing on a planted wrong input before it is trusted (the house rule) — the requirement list has no home for the meta-rule "every check must be able to fail and be shown failing once." R11.2 comes close ("can fail on its own") but doesn't require the failure be demonstrated. Failure caught: a check that always passes (the built check's 0-of-7 backtest). This is my #15 (five dangers — the check's own fault) and #16 (orders clear then enforced). Strong N.

Also note R11.1's "judged by at least one rule..." — "at least one" invites the "at least one" problem; my #14 (numbers not valued for their own sake) and #1 (more 算 wins). Maybe fold into R11.1 change.

Also, "the eleven harness requirements" but the brief numbering lists R1-R10 and R11.1/R11.2 = twelve lines. Verdict per R1–R11.2. I'll give R11 as a pair.

Now, the "can it be checked at readiness" question for each. The definition: readiness proves what is installed and logged in, spends no model quota, marks each line verified/reported/unknown/MISSING. So "checked at readiness" means a no-quota local probe. I'll answer each with: yes (and the check, and how it fails), no (why), or partly (split into a readiness part and a later part).

