Title: # 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — judging a harness-health list before committing to it

Answer (one or two sentences): Keep the list as a checklist of lines to *label*, not as a list of things a config reader can *verify*. Readiness can honestly mark a line `verified`, `reported`, `unknown` or `MISSING`; and only 4 of the 12 can be `verified` there without running the harness. Change R1–R5, R7, R9–R11 to name the observable and the check; split R2, R9, R11.1; keep R6, R8, R13 as safety/authority lines; drop none. Don't enforce the 80% until Q1 and Q2 are answered. My reading: the decisive defect is that the built check's unit is config, and the seven recorded failures were behaviour at dispatch — the check reads the wrong ground (my #17, my #20).

Hmm, "keep R6, R8, R13" — R13 doesn't exist. Let me be careful. Keep R6, R8; R13 is my list item. Let me phrase cleanly.

Let me produce the table.

R1 Clear context delivery — Verdict: Change. Readiness: partly. Check: instruction file exists, is non-empty, and a declared list of on-demand docs/skills resolves to real paths (verified, no quota). Can fail: path missing, empty file, a doc named but absent. Not checkable at readiness: whether the agent actually received it (delivery) or whether the budget was respected — those need a dispatch/observed prompt. Price: a file-exists check passes on a stale or misleading file; "budget as dumping ground" has no comparison, so it must not be given an invented number.

R2 Well-designed tools — Verdict: Split. (a) Interface declaration: every tool has a name, a description, and a stated boundary — readable at readiness, can fail (unnamed/ undocumented tool). (b) Error message that tells how to fix: must be probed with a planted invalid call; that check can fail only after it is shown failing once on a bad call. Price: (b) costs a dispatch for some tools; for local tools, no quota.

R3 Planning and task structure — Verdict: Keep; move the check to step 2. Readiness cannot check done-criteria because tasks don't exist at step 1. Readiness check: a task-list format with a required done-criterion field exists (verified). Step-2 check can fail: a step with no done-criterion. Price: a format can be present and ignored.

R4 State and memory across sessions — Verdict: Change. Readiness: partly. Check: state file exists and its last entry names the next action and the evidence path (no quota, readable). Can fail: last entry names no next action, or points at a step not in the table. Not at readiness: that a fresh context can actually resume — that is a drill, and it is the check that matters (my #4/#16). Price: the drill costs a session.

R5 Independent verification — Verdict: Keep. Readiness: partly. Check: every task names a verifier, and the verifier's command resolves (verified). Can fail: verifier is the builder, or no command. Not at readiness: that the verifier is independent and can actually fail — that is shown by the planted wrong input (my #15). Price: bookkeeping, and a verifier that always passes (the "loving the people" fault).

R6 Guardrails and permissions — Verdict: Keep, and this is the one that may be MUST (safety bound). Readiness: config readable = reported, not verified. Check that can fail: a planted forbidden action (write outside the sandbox path, a denied shell command) is refused and the refusal is logged; also the config key exists. Can fail: action succeeds, or no deny rule. Not at readiness for engines whose config format is unverified (agy/qwen/dsh/opencode) — mark unknown, don't claim. Price: a denial probe must be safe; never edit config (advice only).

R7 Recovery and retries — Verdict: Change. Readiness: can check a declaration, not idempotence. Check: each dispatch declares retry limit, rollback point and re-run safety; a planted failing step exhausts the limit and stops. "Sensible" must be replaced by a comparison or an EJ number — do not invent it. Price: the planted failing step costs a dispatch; without the declaration, retries can loop.

R8 Human-in-the-loop gates — Verdict: Keep. Readiness: config readable = reported. Check that can fail: a planted action that requires approval blocks until approved/refused. On this machine the built check finds the bypass prompt skipped — that is the check failing, and it can fail. Price: a gate made too broad micromanages (EJ's words), a gate too narrow lets a risky call through.

R9 Observability — Verdict: Split. (a) Each dispatch writes one log line with the fields that find a failure: engine, command, exit code, duration, output (or its hash/length), cost. (b) Anomaly detection (empty output, exit 0 with no change, timeout). Readiness: no prior logs, so not verifiable; run one no-quota action and confirm a line appears. Check can fail: a dispatch with no log line, or a log line lacking output. Price: logging output can leak secrets — never print a secret, so record output length/hash or redact.

R10 Feedback loop — Verdict: Keep. Readiness: only that a register exists and is non-empty (reported). Check: every recorded failure has a linked rule/check/tool, and that check is shown failing once. Can fail: a failure with no fix, or a fix with no check. The backtest (0 of 7) is exactly this check returning a bad number. Price: the ledger itself can become a dumping ground (my #1).

R11.1 80% — Verdict: Change (and do not enforce until Q1/Q2 answered and the number has a basis). Readiness: cannot count prompts (they do not exist); readiness can only list the judges the project has (my #1: count your own resources). The count belongs at step 2's gate. Check that can fail: a step (or criterion, per Q1) with no named pre-existing judge. 80% has no basis (n=0) — treat as a target, comparison over time, not a gate. Price: a bare percentage invites "at least one" box-ticking, and my #14: numbers are not valued for their own sake.

R11.2 first signal — Verdict: Keep. Readiness: the declaration (first check exists, and its expected duration, and the reason if not) is readable at readiness — verified as a plan. Whether the first check actually runs first and is faster is only shown by running. Check that can fail: a judge producing the full verdict with no first check; a first check that cannot fail on its own; a first check slower than the thing judged. Price: an extra hop; a first check that never fails is a fault (my #15).

