Steps:
1. my #1 — reckon before battle: a requirement is a claim of advantage; count what you have before you commit. Readiness is the temple reckoning: five matters and seven comparisons map to "what is installed / what can I run now". So first I asked, per line, what evidence exists at readiness.
2. my #2 — make yourself unbeatable first: readiness can only secure what is in your own hands (files, commands, config reads); it cannot make an engine behave. So any line that depends on the engine's behavior cannot be "verified" at readiness; the design must not rest on "they will not fail".
3. my #4 — know the other and yourself: the "other" here is the failure mode. Five of the seven failures came from not knowing the other (dispatch behavior).
4. my #5 — no move without advantage: keep/drop each line by the failure it catches, not by how good it sounds. A line with no failure it catches is a cost with no gain.
5. my #6 — mix harm: for each line, the harm (bookkeeping, a dispatch, a false pass) is stated with its benefit.
6. my #7 and #17 — deception and signs: the seven failures exited 0. Exit code is not a sign; output is. This drove N1 and the split of R9.
7. my #8 — speed, victory not duration: R11.2's first signal is right; a check that costs more than the thing it judges blunts the force.
8. my #11 — foreknowledge must come from people, not from 度: a config file is 度/measurement; the true situation at dispatch is the thing to be learned from probes and logs. This drove "config reads are `reported`, never `verified`", and the refusal to count a document as a judge (Q2).
9. my #13 — not every road is taken: neither readiness nor the health check should claim a line it cannot prove; the honest output is `unknown`/`MISSING`, and it should decline the unprovable road rather than take it.
10. my #14 — ratio of forces: spend the check in proportion to the failure; don't send a 1:1 probe at a 10:1 risk; don't count "more judges" as the goal.
11. my #15 — the general's faults: each check has a virtue that is a handle — the over-cautious check that never fails ("loving the people"), the check set on dying that always fails. This drove the "shown failing once" requirement (N7) and R5.
12. my #16 — orders clear first: Q1 and Q2 are unclear orders; until they are clear, the check cannot be held against anyone. This is why I do not enforce the 80%.
13. my #19 — name the ground, then act as prescribed: readiness's four labels are the ground; my change is that each label must carry a prescription (N4), and the list is a condition→response table, not a sequence.
14. my #20 — judge by the land unit and the tax, and note the closing line is the king's: the unit of measure decides what you see; config was the wrong unit (my reading of the backtest), and the requirements' author is unknown so their words are `attributed`, not a standard (my reading).

