Q1 answer: conditional. If the failure you want to catch is "a result reached the human unjudged", count acceptance criteria, because pass/fail attaches to a criterion, and a prompt with one judge over five criteria would otherwise score as judged when four are not. If the failure is "a prompt was sent with no judge attached", count prompts, because that is the unit the method's step 2 already numbers. The two differ, so pick by the failure; my reading is criteria, because it is the finer unit and finds more unjudged results (my #14: numbers are not the goal; the goal is finding the gap). It makes the number fall and adds bookkeeping. Which failure do you want the 80% to catch?

Q2 answer: only something that can return pass or fail. A design-standard document is 度 — measurement, not testimony — until a named person or rule reads a result against it and can say fail; my #11 `quoted`: 「先知者，不可取於鬼神，不可象於事，不可驗於度，必取於人，知敵之情者也。」 A document that cannot fail is context (R1), not a judge. So: a standard counts only when paired with a named applier and a failing input; otherwise the 80% counts a shelf, not a judge.

